## Economics

Two revenue streams, tracked separately and combined in Analytics against lead cost, Retell and Twilio spend:

- **Outside buyers:** paid per qualified transfer (call reaches that buyer's buffer, e.g. 120 or 220 seconds), whether or not the customer buys. Rate per buyer set in Settings; shown as Expected until I record the buyer's payment.
- **Our own licensed agents:** paid per policy through carrier commission (about **$110 per closed policy** in my case), with advances and chargeback risk if the policy lapses early.

Costs:

- Retell at 400 dials/day: roughly $465–815/month depending on the model. After a transfer the AI fee stops; Retell's telephony fee continues for the transferred conversation.
- Twilio: bills both legs while a verifier/agent/buyer conversation runs; each external transfer's cost is shown per buyer (real Twilio cost when available, otherwise labelled estimate). Recording, if switched on, adds a small per-minute charge.
- **Leads dominate.** 400 dials/day at ~3 attempts per lead is ~2,900 fresh leads/month; at $10 each ~$29,000/month. Bad-lead credits from vendors reduce this — Analytics shows net cost per lead after credits.

Scraping my own leads was advised against (no consent, $500–1,500 per call penalties). Legitimate cheaper routes: my own Facebook lead forms (human-dial-only unless the wording covers AI calls), shared or aged leads with consent proof, bulk negotiation.

## Legal framing I've already been told

Claude is not my lawyer and should keep saying so.

- AI-voice calls fall under the TCPA's rules for artificial/prerecorded voices: prior express written consent is needed for cell numbers, and consent given to a lead generator generally doesn't transfer to me. Purchased and aged lists are the risky case.
- Human-dialled calls (verifier/agent) are not AI-voice calls, but the national DNC list and calling hours still apply.
- Calling someone who asked not to be called is one of the most litigated cases; penalties are $500–1,500 per call.
- Recording: most states are one-party consent, but all-party states (e.g. California, Florida, Illinois, Pennsylvania, Washington) require telling everyone on the call. Recording without the notice there can be a crime.
- Selling final expense requires a licence with Life line of authority in the customer's state.
- The FCC Reassigned Numbers Database can show that a consenting number now belongs to someone else (not built; would need my own registration).
- I need documented consent per lead, monthly DNC refreshes, and an attorney's review before running a real campaign.

